Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

> > > One important thing to note is that an Eigen matmult operation is significantly faster than the cwise -> sum alternative. In high dimensional cases you would definitely want to use matmult.

In the simple example shown below where a 1e4 x 1e4 dimensional matrix is multiplied by a 1e4 dimensional vector the cwise -> sum equivalent was around 7 times slower. The explicit cwise operation was faster than the array casting method, which seems to have additional overhead.  Note, the discrepancies increase with the object dimensions.

auto t1 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now(); Y_i = X_ij * B_j; auto t2 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now(); auto time_matmult = std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::milliseconds>( t2 - t1 ).count(); auto t3 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now(); for (int i=0; i<I; i++) { Y_i(i) = (X_ij.row(i).array() * Btrans_j.array()).sum(); } auto t4 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now(); auto time_cwise1 = std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::milliseconds>( t4 - t3 ).count(); auto t5 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now(); for (int i=0; i<I; i++) { Y_i(i) = (X_ij.row(i).cwiseProduct(Btrans_j)).sum(); } auto t6 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now(); auto time_cwise2 = std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::milliseconds>( t6 - t5 ).count();

time_matmult: 1106 milliseconds
time_cwise1: 7158 milliseconds
time_cwise2: 6608 milliseconds

